---
title: Matzo ball soup
servings: 10
yield: 10 to 12 servings
tags: [jewish, chicken, passover, holiday, make-ahead]
cuisine: jewish
time: 5 hours 30 minutes
source: Matzo Ball Soup by Jennifer Segal, Once Upon a Chef, https://www.onceuponachef.com/recipes/matzo-ball-soup.html
---

== Broth ==

Put the @chicken{1}(whole, 4 to 5 lb, giblets removed), @yellow onions{3}(medium, quartered), @turnips{3}(peeled and quartered, optional), @carrots{6}(roughly chopped) and @celery{5%stalks}(with leaves, roughly chopped) in a #stockpot{}(12-quart). Add about @water{6%l} and bring to a boil.

Boil gently, uncovered, ~{20%minutes}, skimming off the foam.

Turn the heat to low. Add the @bay leaves{2}, @parsley{10%sprigs}, @celery seed{1/4%tsp}, @kosher salt{1%tsp} and @ground white pepper{1/4%tsp}. Cover and simmer ~{3.5%hours}.

Let the pot cool on the stove until no longer hot, then refrigerate overnight.

The next day, skim off most of the fat, leaving a little. Lift out the chicken and vegetables and throw them away. Strain the broth through a #fine-mesh strainer{} into a large bowl or clean pot.

== Matzo balls ==

Stir the @vegetable oil{1/2%cup} and @eggs{4}(large) together in a #large bowl{}. Stir in the @matzo ball mix{2%bags}(one box) and let it stand ~{15%minutes}.

Bring a large pot of water to a boil.

With wet hands, roll the mixture into balls the size of a golf ball. Do not pack them.

Drop them into the boiling water, bring back to a boil, then cover tightly and simmer ~{30%minutes}.

Lift them out and let them cool. Cover and refrigerate until needed, or move them straight into the soup.

== To serve ==

Bring the broth to a simmer. Season it with the @chicken bouillon{2%tbsp}(powder), @kosher salt{2%tsp} and @ground white pepper{1/4%tsp}, adding a little at a time and tasting.

Add the @carrots{4}(cut on the diagonal into 1-inch pieces) and the matzo balls. Simmer ~{20-30%minutes}, until the carrots are tender and the balls float.

Ladle into bowls and top with @dill{1/4%cup}(chopped, or parsley).

> Keep the broth and matzo balls apart in the fridge, up to 3 days, or 3 months frozen.
